<div style="background:#fff;"><div class="cap-section" style="padding-top:0;"><div style="margin-bottom:8px;"><div style="margin-bottom:26px;"><h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin:0;" id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2></div>
    <div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius-card);overflow:hidden;background:#fff;">
      <details class="sc-faq" open>
      <summary>What makes MEMS and sensor assembly different?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">The sensing element must continue interacting with its environment after assembly. This requires an attach process and package structure that protect the device while maintaining pressure, optical, gas or other required access.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>What sensor types can Hana assemble?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">MEMS-based pressure, inertial, acoustic and gas sensors, optical proximity, ambient-light, color and time-of-flight sensors, humidity and temperature sensors, and LiDAR sensor packages, in cavity, open-cavity, LCC and custom package formats.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Which sensor packages has Hana built?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">Examples include open-cavity W185QFN LiDAR packages with a glass lid at 10 × 5.2 mm and 27 × 16 mm, a pre-molded W32 DFN oil pressure sensor package made with film-assisted molding, a 5 × 5 mm cavity QFN pressure sensor package with a port hole in the lid, and open-cavity SOIC and 2.5 × 2.5 mm DFN humidity and temperature sensor packages. Sizes and lead counts are listed in the <a href="/capabilities/osat/package-finder?cat=Sensor%20packages" style="color:var(--hana-blue);font-weight:600;">package finder</a>.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Which package suits which sensor?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">It depends on what the sensing element has to reach. Pressure sensors typically use a lidded cavity with a port hole, or a pre-molded body made with film-assisted molding, as in the W32 DFN oil pressure package. Humidity and temperature sensors use an open cavity, and LiDAR packages an open cavity with a glass lid. Optical sensors use clear transfer mold or caps and filters, covered on the <a href="/capabilities/osat/optical-packaging" style="color:var(--hana-blue);font-weight:600;">Clear &amp; Optical Packaging</a> page.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>How do you protect sensor accuracy?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">Low-stress die attach and stress-decoupling adhesives help limit mechanical loading on the sensing element. Attach materials and cure profiles are selected per device, and the process is developed and qualified during first-article builds, with stress and yield data returned in writing. An NDA is signed before any die or CAD data is shared.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Can Hana test and calibrate sensors during production?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">Electrical and functional test can be integrated into the manufacturing process, including gas and MEMS functional test for medical sensor assemblies. Test and calibration requirements are defined per program.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>What quality systems apply?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">ISO 9001 applies group-wide, with ISO 13485 for medical sensors and IATF 16949 for automotive sensors, in ESD-controlled areas meeting ANSI/ESD S20.20.</div>
    </details>
    </div>
